// The agent door's half of the living plan (Jev M2).
//
// Eight operations, and EngineRpcTarget.cs exposes the same eight under camelCase names (plus
// markPlanTargetDone, which this door reaches through model_plan_action's "done" verb rather than as a tool
// of its own). Both call the same IEngine methods, so the Plan page and the assistant cannot be told different
// plans. The reads and the results of a change are sized for an assistant here (PlanReadShape, PlanResultShape);
// the pipe still carries the whole plan and the whole result, because the Studio reads them. No verb is recognised on a door: model_plan_action's five verbs
// are all sorted out in LocalEngine.ModelPlanActionAsync, so neither door can learn a verb the other has
// not.
//
// NAMING. There is already a get_plan on this door and it means something else: the change plan, the batch
// of reviewed edits that propose_plan builds and apply_plan applies. This family is model_plan* so an
// assistant reading the surface can tell the two apart at a glance. docs/redesign/ui-hierarchy.md calls the
// page "Model Plan", so the prefix is also the name of the place.
//
// The copy follows docs/product-copy-style.md: no engine jargon, no em dashes, honest over smooth. Each
// description says what the operation will NOT do, because that is the part an assistant most needs and is
// least able to find out by trying.

using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

namespace Semanticus.Engine
{
    public static partial class McpTools
    {
        [McpServerTool(Name = "get_model_plan"), Description("The plan for the open model: everything worth doing to it, in value order, grouped by goal (Structure, Meaning, Numbers, Trust, Ship). Each item carries the plain reasons it is there, the exact change it would make, what it touches, who does it (runs on its own, needs you, needs your assistant) and where to go to do it by hand. The plan is worked out fresh from the model every time it is asked for, so it can never disagree with the model. Items keep the same id as long as they are about the same thing. With no suggestion service, or on a free session, the same plan comes back in the order the rules found it, with no scores on it; 'orderedBy' says which you got. On Pro the value order is not there at once either: after a change, the first reads come back in rule order while Jev works the plan out ('jevPending' true, and 'orderedBy' says so), and the value order arrives on a later read, once Jev's own rows have landed and the ranking has been asked. Read again rather than assume the order you got is the value order. An item's reasons describe the WORK and never repeat one rule's message once per finding: one sentence for what was found and why it matters, one naming up to four examples and counting the rest, and one for what fixing it earns, however many findings are behind it. An item's 'diff' is the change itself, one line per case; it is EMPTY when the change depends on a choice only the person can make, and 'touches' then says 'no change until you choose'. An empty diff never means there is nothing to do, and a finding restated is never a diff line. Each item's 'tables' says, per table, how many of the objects it touches are in that table, a short phrase naming that table's own cases, and that table's own change lines, so you can answer 'what is left on Sales' without re-reading the whole plan. 'history' is the plan's story, newest last, each entry carrying a kind (drawn, redrawn, done, started, skipped, unskipped, refused, proposed, approved, cancelled), who did it (you, assistant, plan, engine), the item, the revision and the detail lines. Each item also carries 'state' (target, gap, proposed, applied, done, skipped), which is how far along the propose, review, approve road it is, as distinct from 'status', which is whether the work can be acted on; applied means the CHANGE SET landed and done means the GAP is gone, and applied always comes first because nothing stamps done, the absence of the gap does. An item with changes worked out carries 'changeSet': every line with its before, its after, where it came from, and a 'kept' flag you turn on and off with set_plan_item; a 'digest' over the lines AND the model revision they were worked out against, which model_plan_action('approve') makes you quote so you can only ever approve exactly what you read; 'refused', what was looked at and deliberately left out, with the reason; the cleanup row is a suggestion list for keeping the model lean: every unused column is a line, each with a reason, the ones Semanticus suggests arrive kept with suggested: true and the rest arrive dropped, and the person reviews the list before anything is removed; and 'stale' with a plain reason when the model or the file moved after the lines were worked out, in which case the changes are shown and never silently re-applied. Every row also carries three separate facts: 'source' (found, jev, words, you, workflow, repeat), 'doer' (semanticus, you, assistant; Jev is a source and never a doer) and 'stateWord' (done, set_aside, checking, on_it, paused, waiting, needs_you, ready), which the engine works out, with 'stateNote' beside it; also 'doneWhen' and 'doneText' (how done is known), 'objects', 'held', 'order' (your place in the person's order, on their tasks for you), 'note', 'reply', 'claim' and 'originLink'. 'who', 'status' and 'state' stay for one more release and are deprecated. Each row carries reads, writes, footprintKnown and waitsFor; a row with waitsFor waits for those rows. 'assistantQueue' is 'paused' when the person has paused tasks for you. Rows with ids starting 'pt-' are the person's tasks: adding one, pausing, reordering, changing who does it, the switch and notes are theirs to do in Semanticus, and no tool here does them. A plan too large to read in one result comes back short, and no read is ever more than 40,000 characters, on any model: each row with its state and its counts (touches), its detail left out ('detail' lists, per row, whether its detail is shown, how many lines it holds and where its next page starts), what a row waits for as the ids of the first few and a count (waitsForCount), and 'history' cut to the newest few entries with their first lines (detailNote says how many there are in all). On a plan with very many rows the rows themselves are cut to what fits, the reasons first and then the rows at the end of the list, and detailNote says how many are here. Pass item = a row's id to read that row's lines, tables, objects and every row it waits for, a page at a time (offset = that row's detail.next until it is null, max lines per page); item = rows to read the rows a page at a time (offset = the row to start at, max rows); item = history to read the plan's history a page at a time; or item = history/N to read entry N's lines a page at a time, when a history entry ends with '... and N more lines'. A plan that fits comes back whole. Reads only: nothing here changes the model. get_plan is the same changes as one flat change-by-change list, not different work. When a new model is being started from a SQL source, 'startPlan' says where that plan is up to and what to do next; read it with get_start_plan.")]
        public static async Task<ModelPlanView> GetModelPlan(IEngine engine,
            [Description("A row id from this read, to read that row in detail: its lines, its tables, its objects and every row it waits for, a page at a time. Or 'rows' (the rows a page at a time), 'history' (the plan's history a page at a time), or 'history/N' (one history entry's lines a page at a time). Leave empty for the plan.")] string item = null,
            [Description("With item: where this page starts (a row's detail: 0, then that row's detail.next; rows: the row number to start at; history: the entry; history/N: the line)")] int offset = 0,
            [Description("With item: most lines of each list (or most rows, or history entries) on this page (default 40, at most 200). A page also stops at 40,000 characters, so it can hold fewer.")] int max = 0)
            => PlanReadShape.For(await engine.GetModelPlanAsync().ConfigureAwait(false), item, offset, max);

        [McpServerTool(Name = "get_words_queue"), Description("The Plan row 'Give the words to your assistant' from one call, a page at a time: every open description, business-name, synonym and AI-instructions finding on the open model, plus each measure whose format Semanticus cannot prove and leaves for you to choose, ordered by how much each one raises the AI-readiness grade (a finding that holds the grade under a cap comes first, then the most score points; a lift is what that one fix adds alone now). The best-practice format findings that remain (a rule such as INTEGER_FORMATTING or PERCENTAGE_FORMATTING that wants an exact pattern, on a measure the plan will not write a format to itself, and SEM_COLUMN_NEEDS_FORMAT_STRING on a key or date column no readiness rule asks about, which is yours to choose) come last, each with the pattern the rule asks for in its message and no lift: set it with set_measure_format if it suits what the measure holds. Each item names the tool to use (set_description, rename_object, set_synonyms, create_linguistic_schema when the model has no synonym schema yet, set_ai_instructions, set_measure_format, or set_property with FormatString for a key or date column that has no format), the object, what the rule asks, the points it adds; the page's 'grounding' holds each object's DAX, table and sibling names once, under the objectRef of its items. A page holds at most 40 items and at most 40,000 characters of JSON, whichever comes first, so a model with long measures gives fewer per page. Total and Kinds count the whole queue on every page; call again with offset = Next until Next is null. Read only: it writes nothing. Semanticus writes no words itself; you author them, and the Plan checks they land. This list is for a model that exists. A new model being started from a SQL source keeps its own words in get_start_words, and they are written with write_start_words.")]
        public static Task<WordsQueue> GetWordsQueue(IEngine engine,
            [Description("Most items on this page (default 40, at most 100). A page also stops at 40,000 characters, so it can hold fewer.")] int max = LocalEngine.WordsPageDefault,
            [Description("Where the page starts in the whole list: 0, then the Next of the page before")] int offset = 0)
            => engine.GetWordsQueueAsync(LocalEngine.WordsPageSize(max), System.Math.Max(0, offset));

        [McpServerTool(Name = "skip_model_plan_item"), Description("Set one thing on the plan aside, with a reason. It stays on the plan, marked as set aside and showing the reason, so the next person to read it knows it was a decision rather than something missed. The reason is required. Kept beside the model's other settings, so it travels with the project and not with the person. Changes nothing in the model. The answer is short and comes first: 'sentence' (what changed, and what was left out and why), then 'skipped' (what it left out, each with its reason; when that list is too long for the answer, 'skippedNote' counts the rest and says where they are), 'rows' (the new state of the rows it touched), 'readyDigest' (quote it to run the plan again) and 'counts' (rows in each state). It is not the plan: get_model_plan reads that.")]
        public static async Task<PlanActionShort> SkipModelPlanItem(IEngine engine,
            [Description("The item id from get_model_plan, e.g. 'safefix/Visibility'")] string id,
            [Description("Why you are setting it aside, in your own words. Shown on the plan beside the item.")] string reason)
            => PlanResultShape.OfSkip(await engine.SkipModelPlanItemAsync(id, reason, "agent").ConfigureAwait(false), id, true);

        [McpServerTool(Name = "unskip_model_plan_item"), Description("Put an item that was set aside back on the plan as ordinary work, and forget the reason it was set aside. Changes nothing in the model. The answer is short and comes first: 'sentence' (what changed, and what was left out and why), then 'skipped' (what it left out, each with its reason; when that list is too long for the answer, 'skippedNote' counts the rest and says where they are), 'rows' (the new state of the rows it touched), 'readyDigest' (quote it to run the plan again) and 'counts' (rows in each state). It is not the plan: get_model_plan reads that.")]
        public static async Task<PlanActionShort> UnskipModelPlanItem(IEngine engine,
            [Description("The item id from get_model_plan")] string id)
            => PlanResultShape.OfSkip(await engine.UnskipModelPlanItemAsync(id, "agent").ConfigureAwait(false), id, false);

        [McpServerTool(Name = "model_plan_action"), Description("Act on one item of the model plan. Five actions. 'propose' works out the exact changes for that item and attaches them to it, and WRITES NOTHING: the row then carries every line with its before and after, a digest over them, and the model revision they were worked out against (get_model_plan with item = the row's id reads them); this answer carries the row's state and that digest. 'approve' applies the lines that are kept, all of them or none, in one undoable step, and you must quote the digest the plan showed you, so you can only ever approve the exact diff you read; it refuses if the model moved, if the file on disk moved, or if the lines changed after you read them, and if any kept line cannot be made the whole set is put back and nothing is changed. 'cancel' drops the worked-out changes and writes nothing. 'start' is the older single step: run a safe-fix row straight away with no review. 'done' is the person's: marking an aim or a task done is refused to you with a sentence (they do it in Semanticus); finish a task they gave you with complete_request instead. Keep and drop one line with set_plan_item (approved true or false) between propose and approve. Free on every tier: reading, propose, keep, drop and cancel. Semanticus Pro: start, and approve on a row the engine runs for you (who=auto), because that is the same run by another name. Approve on a row that is yours to do is free. Not every item can propose: descriptions are words and go to your assistant, and a row where the rules have an opinion and you have the answer names the page to decide it on instead of pretending to a change. The way back from an approve is one undo step on this session, not a durable checkpoint, and the result says so. The answer is short and comes first: 'sentence' (what changed, and what was left out and why), then 'skipped' (what it left out, each with its reason; when that list is too long for the answer, 'skippedNote' counts the rest and says where they are), 'rows' (the new state of the rows it touched), 'readyDigest' (quote it to run the plan again) and 'counts' (rows in each state). It is not the plan: get_model_plan reads that.")]
        public static async Task<PlanActionShort> ModelPlanAction(IEngine engine,
            [Description("The item id from get_model_plan")] string id,
            [Description("propose | approve | cancel | start | done")] string action = "start",
            [Description("Required for 'approve': the changeSet.digest the plan showed for this item. It covers the lines AND the model revision they were worked out against, so an approve of anything but exactly what you read is refused. Ignored by every other action.")] string digest = null)
            => PlanResultShape.Of(await engine.ModelPlanActionAsync(id, action, "agent", digest).ConfigureAwait(false));

        [McpServerTool(Name = "apply_plan_items"), Description("Run the plan, or apply several reviewed items of the model plan at once, as ONE undoable step. To run the plan, pass no items and the plan's readyDigest from get_model_plan: every row whose doer is semanticus and whose stateWord is ready is applied in plan order, renames last, and a row with no changes worked out yet has them worked out and applied in the same step. It is refused with 'The plan moved since you read it. Read it again.' when the ready rows changed after your read, including a hold, a doer switch or a skip that did not move the model. A row that waits only for rows in the run is applied in the next batch of the same run (up to twenty-five batches), so one call runs every wave; 'waves' says how many batches ran, 'entryId' is the History entry of the first batch (undo_change with it as toEntry takes the whole run back), and 'canSave' says the model now has changes that are not saved (a run never saves by itself; save_model does). The cleanup row is the person's, so it is never in the ready set, and a cleanup line that is ticked only as a suggestion (its changeSet line says suggested: true) is never applied here until the person ticks, unticks or edits it. A row it cannot take (changes out of date, every change dropped) and a removal whose object is in use by the time the batch reaches it are left out and named in 'skipped'; the rest applies. Otherwise pass every item you want applied with the changeSet.digest the plan showed for it. Every kept line of every listed item goes in one batch: one undo puts all of them back. It is all or nothing across the whole list: if any item's digest is not the one on the plan, if any item is out of date, or if any kept line cannot be made, nothing is changed and the reply names the item and the line that stopped it. The one exception is a removal whose object is in use by the time the batch reaches it: it is left out and named in 'skipped', and the rest applies. An item whose changes are already in is not applied a second time. Lines you dropped are left out, as with model_plan_action('approve'), and an item with every line dropped adds nothing. Semanticus Pro: applying several items at once is running the plan, so a free session is refused and nothing is changed. On free, approve each item on its own with model_plan_action('approve'), which is free for a row that is yours or your assistant's to do and is the same check on one item. The answer is short and comes first: 'sentence' (what changed, and what was left out and why), then 'skipped' (what it left out, each with its reason; when that list is too long for the answer, 'skippedNote' counts the rest and says where they are), 'rows' (the new state of the rows it touched), 'readyDigest' (quote it to run the plan again) and 'counts' (rows in each state). It is not the plan: get_model_plan reads that.")]
        public static async Task<PlanActionShort> ApplyPlanItems(IEngine engine,
            [Description("The items to apply: each is { id, digest }, the item id from get_model_plan and the changeSet.digest it showed. Leave empty to run the plan with readyDigest.")] PlanItemDigest[] items = null,
            [Description("The readyDigest get_model_plan showed. With no items, runs every row that is ready for Semanticus, and is refused if the ready rows moved since that read.")] string readyDigest = null)
            => PlanResultShape.Of(await engine.ApplyPlanItemsAsync(items, "agent", readyDigest).ConfigureAwait(false));

        [McpServerTool(Name = "add_plan_target"), Description("Refused to you: aims and tasks are the person's to add, remove and mark done, so this answers with that sentence and changes nothing. Ask the person to add it in Semanticus. What it does from their side: add one line to what this model should become. The plan already carries an aim per goal read off the rules; this adds the person's own, in their own words, and it is free on every tier because a target is their intent and not work the product did for them. The target appears in 'targets' on get_model_plan marked source 'you', AND as one item in the section you file it under, in state 'target', owned by you, with the sub line 'nothing exists yet'. That item's diff is EMPTY on purpose: nothing in the model matches the target yet, so the plan does not know the change and will not invent one. Where the item points is worked out from your own words by the same local word matching route_ask uses, and when the words name no clear place the item carries no link rather than a guessed one. Typing the same target twice is one target, refiled under whichever goal you named last. Changes nothing in the model. Kept beside the model's other settings, so it travels with the project; a model that was never saved and is not live has nowhere to keep it and says so.")]
        public static Task<ModelPlanView> AddPlanTarget(IEngine engine,
            [Description("What this model should become, in your own words, one line, e.g. 'every measure has a description and a check'")] string text,
            [Description("Which goal it serves: Structure, Meaning, Numbers, Trust or Ship")] string section)
            => engine.AddPlanTargetAsync(text, section, "agent");

        [McpServerTool(Name = "remove_plan_target"), Description("Refused to you: aims and tasks are the person's to add, remove and mark done, so this answers with that sentence and changes nothing. What it does from their side: take one target they added off the plan, and the item it became with it. Only the targets marked source 'you' can be removed; the aim per goal that comes from the rules is derived from the model on every call and is not yours or anyone's to delete. Takes either the target's own id or the id of the item it became, because you are shown both. Free, and it changes nothing in the model. To say you finished a target rather than that you no longer want it, use model_plan_action with action 'done' instead, which leaves it on the list marked done.")]
        public static Task<ModelPlanView> RemovePlanTarget(IEngine engine,
            [Description("The target id from 'targets' on get_model_plan, or the item id, e.g. 'target/every-measure-has-a-check'")] string id)
            => engine.RemovePlanTargetAsync(id, "agent");

        [McpServerTool(Name = "get_next_actions"), Description("The three most valuable things to do to this model right now: the top of the plan, with the same reasons and the same ids. This is the same list get_model_summary shows under its suggestions, so the two can never disagree. Reads only.")]
        public static Task<NextAction[]> GetNextActions(IEngine engine)
            => engine.GetNextActionsAsync();

        [McpServerTool(Name = "route_ask"), Description("Work out which part of Semanticus answers a question asked in ordinary words, and answer the question itself when the engine's own numbers already do. It only ever names a place to go; it never runs anything and never changes anything. The FIRST PASS IS WORD MATCHING and NEEDS NO PERMISSION: the plain nouns and verbs each page is about are matched locally, nothing is sent anywhere, and a clear winner is answered on the spot with 'asked' false. Only a question the words cannot place is sent on, and only then does permission for this model matter. When a create word is recognised, 'create' names the thing being made. The words place a line on 'askdata' (Ask your data, where ask_data answers it) with no permission needed when it splits a number by something ('units per brand', 'customer count by city') or uses the page's own words ('total sales by region', 'top 10 products'); a line that only names a field ('what were sales last month') is placed only when permission lets the service be asked, and otherwise reads the no-match line. The same words can be a task and a question, and nothing here decides that for the person: whenever a line is placed on 'askdata', the answer carries BOTH readings and neither is hidden. 'reading' names the top one, 'task' or 'question', and its place is on 'tool', 'area', 'confidence' and 'sentence' as usual; 'other' carries the second one, with its own 'kind', 'tool', 'area', 'confidence' and 'line' to show. ORDER: the question reading is first only when the line plainly asks, which is read from the words of the line alone and never from what placed it. A line plainly asks when it ends in a question mark, opens with what, which, how, why, when, who or where (after a softening 'please', 'can you' or 'could we'), opens with 'show me' or 'list', or opens by asking for Ask your data by name ('ask your data please', 'go to ask your data'). Every other line reads the task first with Ask your data as the named second choice, the examples above included: 'total sales by region' reads task first. The task reading names the page the words say the work is done on, or no page at all, which is ordinary for a task and is exactly what the Plan's row shows for the same words; the person's Enter in the Plan's field keeps the words as a task whichever reading came first. CONFIDENCE belongs to the reading it sits on: 'confidence' is the top reading's and 'other.confidence' the second one's. A reading the words or the engine's own numbers decided is 1, a reading the service decided carries the service's own number, and a task reading that names no page is 0. The service decides only the page it names: one page other than Ask your data (a single reading, on top), or the question reading on Ask your data, which sits on top or on 'other' by the order rule. So where 'asked' is true and the question is second, the service's number is 'other.confidence' and the top reads no tool at 0; that answer has a 'reading', which a line the service left unplaced does not. When nothing matches, 'fallback' is 'menu', 'sentence' is the plain line a person is shown and 'reason' carries why it went no further, which is for you and not for them. This is a Semanticus Pro feature; a session without Pro is refused before any word is matched.")]
        public static Task<AskRouteResult> RouteAsk(IEngine engine,
            [Description("What the person typed, in their own words, e.g. 'why is my margin measure wrong'")] string text)
            => engine.RouteAskAsync(text);
    }
}
